Subject: [PATCH 2/4] regalloc: gate XMM pool on 64-bit target (i686 got
 phantom XMM homes)

The x86-64 detection `available_regs.iter().any(|r| r.0 == 1)` tested
for rbx=PhysReg(1) -- but i686's callee-saved pool is ebx/esi/edi/ebp =
PhysReg(0..3), where PhysReg(1) is %esi. Every i686 function therefore
passed the check and the Phase-3 F64 scan handed out XMM PhysRegs
(20..33) that the i686 backend cannot emit: the slot assigner saw an
assignment and skipped the stack slot, so every consumer read a
nonexistent home.

Runtime reproducer fptest.c (double accumulate loop, i386 nostdlib
harness): O2 returned garbage (exit 1) where GCC returns 23.0; with
CCC_NO_XMM_REGALLOC=1 it passed -- pinpointing the phantom pool. After
gating on !target_is_32bit(): exit 0.

Also prepares I686_CALLER_SAVED for the upcoming ecx/edx phase-2 work
(names for PhysReg 4/5 in phys_reg_name; pool still empty until the
hazard scan lands).

Validation: cargo test 687; regression 209; correctness 50; linker 86;
intrinsics 3; asm-diff exact; fuzz 300/300 (O2+Os); fptest O2 exit 0.
