Subject: [PATCH 4/4] i686 regalloc: ecx/edx caller-saved allocation with
 per-register hazard scan

THE big size lever for the 32 KiB kernel setup binary: i686 had ZERO
caller-saved registers (I686_CALLER_SAVED = []), so every intermediate
that outlives one instruction went to a stack slot or a callee-saved
register with its push/pop tax. GCC's main advantage on this code is
exactly its free use of ecx/edx between calls.

Design (fail-closed):
* collect_i686_scratch_hazard_points() walks the IR and returns, per
  register, the sorted program points where the i686 EMITTERS themselves
  may clobber %ecx or %edx (operand_to_ecx staging for value-rhs binops/
  cmps, cltd/idivl for div/rem, indirect load/store pointers, wide-copy
  high halves, emit_save_acc, %cl shifts, PIC switch tables, compare-
  and-branch FUSION re-emitting the cmp at the CondBranch, wide returns).
  It is a WHITELIST over instructions audited in the i686 emitters: any
  unlisted construct is a hazard for both registers -- a miss costs a
  register, never a value.
* Phase 2 splits into per-register scans (edx first: fewer hazards)
  filtered by 'interval spans no hazard point of that register', reusing
  the sorted-points binary search from spans_any_call. 64-bit backends
  keep the single unfiltered scan (their pools never contain PhysReg 4/5).
* PhysReg(4)=ecx, PhysReg(5)=edx added to phys_reg_name.

